/-!
# Smooth rational models and comparison of regular maps

Source: Bierstone–Milman–Temkin, Q-universal desingularization, Definition 7.2,
proof of Theorem 7.4, and Theorem 7.5, pp. 18–21, arXiv:0905.3580v1
(blueprint: lem-per-variety-rational-smooth-limit-models).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- An affine finite disjoint union of generically reduced characteristic-zero varieties
has a rational affine smooth-transition presentation with regular, schematically dominant
projections. The fields may differ and the finite disjoint union may be empty.
Source: Bierstone–Milman–Temkin, Definition 7.2 and Theorem 7.5, pp. 18–21
(blueprint: lem-per-variety-rational-smooth-limit-models).

Proof of both statements in this module (the node's full proof):
1. First treat $X=\operatorname{Spec}A$ of finite type over one characteristic-zero field $k$.
Since a field is Noetherian (its only ideals are zero and itself), the cited finite-type and
finite-presentation lemmas give $A=k[x_1,\ldots,x_n]/(f_1,\ldots,f_m)$. Let $R_0\subset k$ be the
finitely generated rational subalgebra generated by the coefficients of these finitely many
polynomials and put $B_0=R_0[x]/(f)$. The maps sending each coefficient and variable to itself
give mutually inverse maps $k\otimes_{R_0}B_0\simeq A$ by the tensor pushout property. Generic
flatness gives $a\ne0$ in $R_0$ with $(B_0)_a$ flat over $R_0$; the localization flatness lemma
makes it flat over $(R_0)_a$. Replace $R_0,B_0$ by these localizations, which remain finite type
over $\mathbf Q$ and still give $A$. Thus $R_0\subset k$ is a domain and $B_0$ is flat over it.
Dependencies: lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra-noetherian,
lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-noetherian-finite-presentation,
lem-weil-pbc-algebra-tensor-pushout-universal-property,
lem-sbc-finite-type-algebra-generic-flatness, lem-weil-pbc-flat-over-localization.
2. Let $I$ consist of all finitely generated rational subalgebras $C\subset k$ containing $R_0$,
ordered by $C\preceq D$ when $C\subseteq D$ and $C\to D$ is smooth. This is a small set (a subset
of the power set of $k$), nonempty since it contains $R_0$. Identity maps are smooth directly from
the lifting definition, smooth composition gives transitivity, and antisymmetry follows from
inclusion. Here is the finite refinement argument, not a separate lemma: for $C_1,C_2\in I$ and
finite $F\subset k$, let $S\subset k$ be the algebra generated by $C_1,C_2,F$. Each
$C_t\hookrightarrow S$ is injective and finite type between domains; its fraction-field extension
is separable because it has characteristic zero. Generic smoothness yields $g_t\ne0$ in $S$ with
$C_t\to S_{g_t}$ smooth. Then $D=S_{g_1g_2}\subset k$ is smooth over both $C_t$, by further
principal localization, and contains $F$. Hence $I$ is directed, and its underlying subrings cover
$k$. Dependencies: lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-base-change-composition,
lem-weil-pbc-char-zero-field-extension-separable, lem-exc-generic-smooth-domain,
lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-principal-localization.
3. Put $B_C=C\otimes_{R_0}B_0$ and $X_C=\operatorname{Spec}B_C$. Every $B_C$ is a finite type
rational algebra, hence Noetherian; it is flat over $C$ by flat base change. Apply
[lem-per-flat-model-generic-reducedness] with $R=R_0,D=C,B=B_0$ and the given embedding into $k$:
its field base change is exactly the generically reduced ring $A$, so $B_C$ is generically
reduced. For $C\preceq D$, the identification $B_D=D\otimes_C B_C$, obtained by sending pure
tensors to pure tensors in both directions, identifies $B_C\to B_D$ with a base change of the
smooth map $C\to D$. Thus $X_D\to X_C$ is smooth and affine. Dependencies:
lem-per-flat-model-generic-reducedness, lem-weil-pbc-flat-module-base-change,
lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra-noetherian, lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-base-change-composition,
lem-weil-pbc-algebra-tensor-pushout-universal-property, def-sbc-smooth-morphisms.
4. The compatible maps $B_C\to A$ exhibit $A$ as their colimit: compatible ring maps from all
$B_C$ to a ring $T$ restrict to one map $k\to T$, because the subrings $C$ cover $k$ and every
finite collection is contained in an upper stage; together with the common map $B_0\to T$ the
tensor pushout property gives a unique map $k\otimes_{R_0}B_0\to T$. The affine limit lemma
therefore identifies $X$ with $\varprojlim X_C$. Moreover, tensoring each inclusion
$C\hookrightarrow k$ with the flat $R_0$-module $B_0$ gives an injection $B_C\hookrightarrow A$.
On affine schemes schematic dominance means precisely this injection: the kernel sheaf of
$\mathcal O_{X_C}\to(p_C)_*\mathcal O_X$ is the sheaf associated to the kernel of $B_C\to A$,
which is zero. This proves the optional dominance without a schematic-image replacement.
Dependencies: lem-weil-pbc-algebra-tensor-pushout-universal-property,
lem-weil-pbc-limit-affine-transition, lem-trf-flat-tensor-preserves-injection.
5. We record explicitly the affine ring/scheme translation used below. For $R\to T$ with $T$
Noetherian, every fibre $T\otimes_R\kappa(\mathfrak p)$ is a localization of $T/\mathfrak pT$,
hence Noetherian: ideals of a quotient lift to finitely generated ideals, and localization
preserves Noetherianity. The affine fibre-product identification and the affine flatness criterion
identify flatness and fibres on the two sides. A regular ring map thus gives a regular scheme
morphism, since the cited geometrically-regular extension lemma tests every finite residue-field
extension, not just purely inseparable ones. Conversely a regular scheme morphism tests all finite
extensions and in particular the purely inseparable ones; regularity of its affine fibres says
exactly that the fibre rings are regular. Thus the two meanings of regular coincide here.
Dependencies: lem-weil-pbc-localization-noetherian, lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-spectra,
lem-weil-pbc-flat-ring-map-iff-flat-affine,
lem-weil-pbc-geometrically-regular-finitely-generated-extensions, def-per-regular-scheme-morphism,
def-weil-pbc-g-ring, def-weil-regular-scheme.
6. For fixed $C$, the tail $D\succeq C$ is small nonempty filtered, has the same colimit $A$ (any
stage has a common upper stage with $C$), and each $B_C\to B_D$ is smooth. All fibres of $B_C\to
A$ are Noetherian by step 5 because $A$ is Noetherian. Apply the smooth-colimit regularity lemma
and step 5 to obtain regularity of every $p_C$. The same argument proves that every projection of
ANY directed inverse system of affine finite-type rational schemes with smooth transitions and
Noetherian limit is regular; no injectivity of its transition ring maps is needed. Dependencies:
lem-per-smooth-colimit-regular, lem-weil-pbc-limit-affine-transition.
7. For finitely many field components use the product of their nonempty directed index sets, and
at each stage the finite disjoint union of the corresponding models. A finite disjoint union of
affine schemes is affine with coordinate ring the finite product: the standard orthogonal
idempotents split its spectrum into precisely the component spectra. The resulting ring colimit is
the finite product of the component colimits, since representatives and all finitely many
equalities can be moved to one common tuple of upper indices. Hence the affine limit is the
required disjoint union. Smoothness, generic reducedness, schematic dominance and regularity are
checked separately on these open-and-closed components, using the stalk/fibre definitions (and
componentwise injectivity for dominance). For the empty scheme take the constant empty system over
a singleton. This also treats any empty components. Dependencies:
lem-weil-pbc-limit-affine-transition, def-sbc-smooth-morphisms, def-per-regular-scheme-morphism,
def-pc-reduced-scheme.
8. Now take the two arbitrary presentations in the comparison assertion. Write
$A_i=\Gamma(X_i,\mathcal O)$, $B_j=\Gamma(Y_j,\mathcal O)$, $A=\Gamma(X,\mathcal O)$ and
$B=\Gamma(Y,\mathcal O)$. The affine limit lemma identifies their ring colimits with $A,B$. Every
morphism between rational schemes respects their rational structure maps: a unital ring map fixes
integers and inverses of nonzero integers. The finite rational algebra $A_i$ is finitely presented
since $\mathbf Q$ is Noetherian. Finite-presentation factorization applied to $A_i\to A\to
B=\varinjlim B_j$ gives $A_i\to B_{j_1}$. Choose a common upper bound $j\ge j_1,j_0$; compose to
obtain $g:Y_j\to X_i$ with $gq_j=p_i h$. At this point $g$ is only finite type: a finite set of
rational algebra generators of $B_j$ also generates it as an $A_i$-algebra. Dependencies:
lem-weil-pbc-limit-affine-transition, lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-noetherian-finite-presentation,
lem-weil-pbc-finite-presentation-algebra-factors-filtered-colimit.
9. By step 6 the projection $X\to X_i$ is regular. By hypothesis $h$ is regular. Translate them to
ring maps by step 5, and apply regular composition to $A_i\to A\to B$: every composite fibre is
Noetherian because it is a quotient followed by a localization of the Noetherian ring $B$. For
$y\in Y$, put $z=q_j(y)$, $x=g(z)$. The local criterion for regular maps gives regularity of
$\mathcal O_{X_i,x}\to\mathcal O_{Y,y}$ and of $\mathcal O_{Y_j,z}\to\mathcal O_{Y,y}$, the latter
from regularity of $q_j$. This latter map is flat and local, hence faithfully flat and surjective
on spectra. Regular permanence for the local triangle therefore proves that $\mathcal
O_{X_i,x}\to\mathcal O_{Y_j,z}$ is regular. Notice that this conclusion is asserted only at points
in the image of $Y$. Dependencies: lem-weil-pbc-regular-composition,
lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-maximal, lem-weil-pbc-flat-local-hom-faithfully-flat,
lem-weil-pbc-regular-permanence.
10. This makes $g$ smooth at $z$. Indeed let $P\subset A_i,Q\subset B_j$ correspond to $x,z$, and
$F=B_j\otimes_{A_i}\kappa(P)$ with prime $Q_F$ over $Q$. The fibre-local-ring isomorphism
identifies $F_{Q_F}$ with $(B_j)_Q/P(B_j)_Q$, which is regular local by regularity of
$(A_i)_P\to(B_j)_Q$. The finite type $\kappa(P)$-algebra $F$ has separable residue extension at
$Q_F$, since its fields have characteristic zero. The regular-local/separable-residue smoothness
criterion gives smoothness of $\kappa(P)\to F$ there. The flat-smooth-fibre criterion now applies:
$A_i\to B_j$ is finitely presented by the Noetherian finite-type lemma (take its element $g=1$),
and its local map at $Q$ is flat because it is regular. Thus $A_i\to B_j$ is smooth at $Q$.
Dependencies: lem-weil-pbc-fibre-ring-local-ring-iso,
lem-weil-pbc-char-zero-field-extension-separable,
lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-separable-residue-smooth,
lem-weil-pbc-flat-smooth-fibre-smooth-at-prime,
lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-noetherian-finite-presentation.
11. Let $U\subset Y_j$ be the smooth locus of $g$, the union of the principal opens specified by
the singular-ideal node. Step 10 gives $q_j(Y)\subset U$. Write its closed complement as
$Z=\operatorname{Spec}(B_j/H_{B_j/A_i})$. On the directed tail $k\ge j$, form
$Z_k=Y_k\times_{Y_j}Z$, affine and quasi-compact. Their limit is empty: on rings it is
$B\otimes_{B_j}(B_j/H)=B/HB$, by the tensor pushout property and the colimit universal property;
its spectrum is the inverse image of $Z$, which has no points. By the nonempty-limit lemma, not
all $Z_k$ can be nonempty. Choose $k\ge j$ with $Z_k$ empty. Thus the transition $Y_k\to Y_j$ has
image inside $U$ and factors uniquely through it. This argument includes $Y$ empty and does not
assume that the limit projection is surjective. Dependencies: def-weil-pbc-singular-ideal,
lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-spectra, lem-weil-pbc-algebra-tensor-pushout-universal-property,
lem-weil-pbc-limit-affine-transition, lem-weil-pbc-limit-nonempty,
lem-weil-pbc-morphism-factors-through-open-subscheme.
12. The transition $Y_k\to Y_j$ is smooth by assumption, so its factor $Y_k\to U$ is smooth by
restriction to an open target. By construction $g|_U:U\to X_i$ is smooth: its principal open cover
consists of smooth rational algebra maps. Their composite $h_{ki}:Y_k\to U\to X_i$ is smooth,
using composition on affine charts. The defining factorization and transition equations give
$h_{ki}q_k=gq_j=p_i h$, and $k\ge j_0$. Finally take $h=\mathrm{id}$ to obtain the assertion about
two presentations; identities are flat and their fibres and finite field base changes are spectra
of fields, so they are regular. All uses of rational coefficients above come from the unique
rational structure map, with no compatibility between the original ground fields required.
Dependencies: lem-weil-pbc-smooth-restrict-open-subschemes,
lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-base-change-composition, def-sbc-smooth-morphisms,
def-per-regular-scheme-morphism.
-/
theorem exists_rational_smooth_limit_models
    (X : Scheme.{u}) [IsAffine X]
    (A : Type u) [Finite A] (C : A → Scheme.{u}) (ι : ∀ a : A, C a ⟶ X)
    (hc : IsColimit (Cofan.mk X ι))
    (K : A → Type u) [∀ a : A, Field (K a)] [∀ a : A, CharZero (K a)]
    (s : ∀ a : A, C a ⟶ Spec (CommRingCat.of (K a)))
    (hft : ∀ a : A, LocallyOfFiniteType (s a) ∧ QuasiCompact (s a))
    (hred : ∀ a : A, IsGenericallyReduced (C a)) :
    ∃ (I : Type u) (_ : Preorder I) (_ : IsDirected I (· ≤ ·)) (_ : Nonempty I)
      (D : Iᵒᵖ ⥤ Scheme.{u}) (p : (Functor.const Iᵒᵖ).obj X ⟶ D),
      Nonempty (IsLimit (Cone.mk X p)) ∧
      (∀ i : Iᵒᵖ, IsAffine (D.obj i) ∧ IsGenericallyReduced (D.obj i) ∧
        ∃ t : D.obj i ⟶ Spec (CommRingCat.of (ULift.{u} ℚ)),
          LocallyOfFiniteType t ∧ QuasiCompact t) ∧
      (∀ (i j : Iᵒᵖ) (f : i ⟶ j), Smooth (D.map f) ∧ IsAffineHom (D.map f)) ∧
      (∀ i : Iᵒᵖ, IsRegularSchemeMorphism (p.app i) ∧
        IsSchemeTheoreticallyDominant (p.app i)) := by
  sorry

/-- A regular morphism between Noetherian limits of arbitrary affine finite-type rational
smooth-transition systems descends, above any prescribed source stage, to a smooth map.
In particular, taking the identity compares any two presentations of the same scheme.
There is no compatibility hypothesis on characteristic-zero ground fields, no generic
reducedness hypothesis on these arbitrary systems, and no dominance hypothesis.
Source: Bierstone–Milman–Temkin, proof of Theorem 7.4, p. 19
(blueprint: lem-per-variety-rational-smooth-limit-models).

Proof: apply steps 5–6 and 8–12 of the full proof above. The projections are regular by
smooth-colimit regularity. Finite presentation first factors the map through a finite
source stage above the prescribed stage. Regular permanence for the local triangle makes
this factorization smooth on the image of the limit. The closed nonsmooth locus has empty
inverse limit; the nonempty-limit lemma makes its inverse image empty at some later stage.
The later transition therefore factors through the smooth locus, and restriction and
composition of smooth morphisms give the required smooth factorization. The cone equations
give the displayed equality. Applying this to the identity gives presentation independence.
-/
theorem regular_hom_descends_to_smooth_stage
    {I J : Type u} [Preorder I] [IsDirected I (· ≤ ·)] [Nonempty I]
    [Preorder J] [IsDirected J (· ≤ ·)] [Nonempty J]
    (D : Iᵒᵖ ⥤ Scheme.{u}) (E : Jᵒᵖ ⥤ Scheme.{u})
    (hD : ∀ i : Iᵒᵖ, IsAffine (D.obj i) ∧
      ∃ t : D.obj i ⟶ Spec (CommRingCat.of (ULift.{u} ℚ)),
        LocallyOfFiniteType t ∧ QuasiCompact t)
    (hE : ∀ j : Jᵒᵖ, IsAffine (E.obj j) ∧
      ∃ t : E.obj j ⟶ Spec (CommRingCat.of (ULift.{u} ℚ)),
        LocallyOfFiniteType t ∧ QuasiCompact t)
    (hDt : ∀ (i i' : Iᵒᵖ) (f : i ⟶ i'), Smooth (D.map f) ∧ IsAffineHom (D.map f))
    (hEt : ∀ (j j' : Jᵒᵖ) (f : j ⟶ j'), Smooth (E.map f) ∧ IsAffineHom (E.map f))
    (c : Cone D) (d : Cone E) (hc : IsLimit c) (hd : IsLimit d)
    [IsNoetherian c.pt] [IsNoetherian d.pt]
    (h : d.pt ⟶ c.pt) (hh : IsRegularSchemeMorphism h) (i : I) (j₀ : J) :
    ∃ j : J, j₀ ≤ j ∧ ∃ hji : E.obj (op j) ⟶ D.obj (op i),
      Smooth hji ∧ h ≫ c.π.app (op i) = d.π.app (op j) ≫ hji := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
